WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.717
Scans, filters and cursors showed
how to choose what to read.

2
00:00:04.717 --> 00:00:09.073
Here, we ask what happens
when one change touches several keys.

3
00:00:09.423 --> 00:00:14.834
This is Atomic writes: related changes
become visible as a whole, or not at all.

4
00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:20.298
Suppose an order changes two keys:
its status and its tracking reference.

5
00:00:20.298 --> 00:00:24.089
They belong together; once one is live,
the other must be too.

6
00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:27.290
Two ordinary writes are
two chances to stop.

7
00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:30.790
If the second fails,
the first may already be visible.

8
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:34.704
A non-atomic batch remains useful
when throughput matters

9
00:00:34.704 --> 00:00:37.080
and partial application is acceptable.

10
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.893
It does not make those
writes all-or-nothing.

11
00:00:40.893 --> 00:00:44.504
For a batch in one tree,
use the atomic write surface.

12
00:00:44.854 --> 00:00:47.240
On success, the whole batch commits.

13
00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.820
If the saga aborts,
none of its writes become visible.

14
00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.466
A timeout alone does not tell
you whether it committed.

15
00:00:54.816 --> 00:00:56.996
Readers do not see half of this batch.

16
00:00:56.996 --> 00:01:00.265
Its prepared writes stay hidden
until one commit decision

17
00:01:00.265 --> 00:01:01.871
makes them visible together.

18
00:01:02.221 --> 00:01:04.315
An abort drops those hidden writes;

19
00:01:04.315 --> 00:01:07.068
it does not need to write
the old values back.

20
00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:11.034
Sometimes one change spans distinct trees:

21
00:01:11.034 --> 00:01:14.424
an order leaves one tree
and appears in another.

22
00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:17.299
The single-tree call cannot cover both.

23
00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:20.924
The cross-tree builder stages
each tree's part under one

24
00:01:20.924 --> 00:01:22.736
stable operation identifier.

25
00:01:23.086 --> 00:01:26.910
One shared decision applies
to every participating tree:

26
00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.095
commit them all, or commit none.

27
00:01:30.095 --> 00:01:34.831
That is atomicity for the write,
not a snapshot read across trees.

28
00:01:35.181 --> 00:01:39.236
Two separate reads at different
moments can straddle the decision:

29
00:01:39.236 --> 00:01:42.738
one may return the old state,
and the next the new state.

30
00:01:44.538 --> 00:01:47.186
Next on Build: TTL and soft delete.

31
00:01:47.186 --> 00:01:52.256
The deep dive on how this works is
Atomic commit without consensus.

32
00:01:52.256 --> 00:01:56.645
The links are on this episode's page
in the documentation.
